  "question": {
    "text": "In Chromyl chloride, the oxidation state of chromium is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mo>+</mo></mfenced></math> ___________.",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "6",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The chemical formula of Chromyl chloride is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>. The oxidation state of chromium in chromyl chloride (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>) is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></math>.</p><p>Let the oxidation number of Chromium be &ldquo;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math>&rdquo;, the oxidation state of oxygen &nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></math>, the oxidation state of chlorine&nbsp; is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></math></p><p>Then,</p><p>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p></blockquote>",
